:" let hello = String::from(\"Hello, world!\");"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" You can append a [`char`] to a `String` with the [`push`] method, and"},{"DocComment":" append a [`&str`] with the [`push_str`] method:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let mut hello = String::from(\"Hello, \");"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" hello.push('w');"},{"DocComment":" hello.push_str(\"orld!\");"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`push`]: String::push"},{"DocComment":" [`push_str`]: String::push_str"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you have a vector of UTF-8 bytes, you can create a `String` from it with"},{"DocComment":" the [`from_utf8`] method:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" // some bytes, in a vector"},{"DocComment":" let sparkle_heart = vec![240, 159, 146, 150];"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // We know these bytes are valid, so we'll use `unwrap()`."},{"DocComment":" let sparkle_heart = String::from_utf8(sparkle_heart).unwrap();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(\"💖\", sparkle_heart);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`from_utf8`]: String::from_utf8"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # UTF-8"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `String`s are always valid UTF-8. If you need a non-UTF-8 string, consider"},{"DocComment":" [`OsString`]. It is similar, but without the UTF-8 constraint. Because UTF-8"},{"DocComment":" is a variable width encoding, `String`s are typically smaller than an array of"},{"DocComment":" the same `char`s:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" // `s` is ASCII which represents each `char` as one byte"},{"DocComment":" let s = \"hello\";"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(s.len(), 5);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // A `char` array with the same contents would be longer because"},{"DocComment":" // every `char` is four bytes"},{"DocComment":" let s = ['h', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'];"},{"DocComment":" let size: usize = s.into_iter().map(|c| size_of_val(&c)).sum();"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size, 20);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // However, for non-ASCII strings, the difference will be smaller"},{"DocComment":" // and sometimes they are the same"},{"DocComment":" let s = \"💖💖💖💖💖\";"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(s.len(), 20);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let s = ['💖', '💖', '💖', '💖', '💖'];"},{"DocComment":" let size: usize = s.into_iter().map(|c| size_of_val(&c)).sum();"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size, 20);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This raises interesting questions as to how `s[i]` should work."},{"DocComment":" What should `i` be here? Several options include byte indices and"},{"DocComment":" `char` indices but, because of UTF-8 encoding, only byte indices"},{"DocComment":" would provide constant time indexing. Getting the `i`th `char`, for"},{"DocComment":" example, is available using [`chars`]:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let s = \"hello\";"},{"DocComment":" let third_character = s.chars().nth(2);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(third_character, Some('l'));"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let s = \"💖💖💖💖💖\";"},{"DocComment":" let third_character = s.chars().nth(2);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(third_character, Some('💖'));"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Next, what should `s[i]` return? Because indexing returns a reference"},{"DocComment":" to underlying data it could be `&u8`, `&[u8]`, or something similar."},{"DocComment":" Since we're only providing one index, `&u8` makes the most sense but that"},{"DocComment":" might not be what the user expects and can be explicitly achieved with"},{"DocComment":" [`as_bytes()`]:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" // The first byte is 104 - the byte value of `'h'`"},{"DocComment":" let s = \"hello\";"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(s.as_bytes()[0], 104);"},{"DocComment":" // or"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(s.as_bytes()[0], b'h');"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // The first byte is 240 which isn't obviously useful"},{"DocComment":" let s = \"💖💖💖💖💖\";"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(s.as_bytes()[0], 240);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Due to these ambiguities/restrictions, indexing with a `usize` is simply"},{"DocComment":" forbidden:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0277"},{"DocComment":" let s = \"hello\";"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // The following will not compile!"},{"DocComment":" println!(\"The first letter of s is {}\", s[0]);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It is more clear, however, how `&s[i..j]` should work (that is,"},{"DocComment":" indexing with a range). It should accept byte indices (to be constant-time)"},{"DocComment":" and return a `&str` which is UTF-8 encoded. This is also called \"string slicing\"."},{"DocComment":" Note this will panic if the byte indices provided are not character"},{"DocComment":" boundaries - see [`is_char_boundary`] for more details. See the implementations"},{"DocComment":" for [`SliceIndex<str>`] for more details on string slicing. For a non-panicking"},{"DocComment":" version of string slicing, see [`get`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`OsString`]: ../../std/ffi/struct.OsString.html \"ffi::OsString\""},{"DocComment":" [`SliceIndex<str>`]: core::slice::SliceIndex"},{"DocComment":" [`as_bytes()`]: str::as_bytes"},{"DocComment":" [`get`]: str::get"},{"DocComment":" [`is_char_boundary`]: str::is_char_boundary"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The [`bytes`] and [`chars`] methods return iterators over the bytes and"},{"DocComment":" codepoints of the string, respectively. To iterate over codepoints along"},{"DocComment":" with byte indices, use [`char_indices`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`bytes`]: str::bytes"},{"DocComment":" [`chars`]: str::chars"},{"DocComment":" [`char_indices`]: str::char_indices"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Deref"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `String` implements <code>[Deref]<Target = [str]></code>, and so inherits all of [`str`]'s"},{"DocComment":" methods. In addition, this means that you can pass a `String` to a"},{"DocComment":" function which takes a [`&str`] by using an ampersand (`&`):"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" fn takes_str(s: &str) { }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let s = String::from(\"Hello\");"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" takes_str(&s);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This will create a [`&str`] from the `String` and pass it in. This"},{"DocComment":" conversion is very inexpensive, and so generally, functions will accept"},{"DocComment":" [`&str`]s as arguments unless they need a `String` for some specific"},{"DocComment":" reason."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In certain cases Rust doesn't have enough information to make this"},{"DocComment":" conversion, known as [`Deref`] coercion. In the following example a string"},{"DocComment":" slice [`&'a str`][`&str`] implements the trait `TraitExample`, and the function"},{"DocComment":" `example_func` takes anything that implements the trait. In this case Rust"},{"DocComment":" would need to make two implicit conversions, which Rust doesn't have the"},{"DocComment":" means to do. For that reason, the following example will not compile."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0277"},{"DocComment":" trait TraitExample {}"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl<'a> TraitExample for &'a str {}"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" fn example_func<A: TraitExample>(example_arg: A) {}"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let example_string = String::from(\"example_string\");"},{"DocComment":" example_func(&example_string);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" There are two options that would work instead. The first would be to"},{"DocComment":" change the line `example_func(&example_string);` to"},{"DocComment":" `example_func(example_string.as_str());`, using the method [`as_str()`]"},{"DocComment":" to explicitly extract the string slice containing the string. The second"},{"DocComment":" way changes `example_func(&example_string);` to"},{"DocComment":" `example_func(&*example_string);`. In this case we are dereferencing a"},{"DocComment":" `String` to a [`str`], then referencing the [`str`] back to"},{"DocComment":" [`&str`]. The second way is more idiomatic, however both work to do the"},{"DocComment":" conversion explicitly rather than relying on the implicit conversion."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Representation"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" A `String` is made up of three components: a pointer to some bytes, a"},{"DocComment":" length, and a capacity. The pointer points to the internal buffer which `String`"},{"DocComment":" uses to store its data. The length is the number of bytes currently stored"},{"DocComment":" in the buffer, and the capacity is the size of the buffer in bytes. As such,"},{"DocComment":" the length will always be less than or equal to the capacity."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This buffer is always stored on the heap."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" You can look at these with the [`as_ptr`], [`len`], and [`capacity`]"},{"DocComment":" methods:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let story = String::from(\"Once upon a time...\");"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Deconstruct the String into parts."},{"DocComment":" let (ptr, len, capacity) = story.into_raw_parts();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // story has nineteen bytes"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(19, len);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // We can re-build a String out of ptr, len, and capacity. This is all"},{"DocComment":" // unsafe because we are responsible for making sure the components are"},{"DocComment":" // valid:"},{"DocComment":" let s = unsafe { String::from_raw_parts(ptr, len, capacity) } ;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(String::from(\"Once upon a time...\"), s);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`as_ptr`]: str::as_ptr"},{"DocComment":" [`len`]: String::len"},{"DocComment":" [`capacity`]: String::capacity"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If a `String` has enough capacity, adding elements to it will not"},{"DocComment":" re-allocate. For example, consider this program:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let mut s = String::new();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" println!(\"{}\", s.capacity());"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" for _ in 0..5 {"},{"DocComment":"     s.push_str(\"hello\");"},{"DocComment":"     println!(\"{}\", s.capacity());"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This will output the following:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```text"},{"DocComment":" 0"},{"DocComment":" 8"},{"DocComment":" 16"},{"DocComment":" 16"},{"DocComment":" 32"},{"DocComment":" 32"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" At first, we have no memory allocated at all, but as we append to the"},{"DocComment":" string, it increases its capacity appropriately. If we instead use the"},{"DocComment":" [`with_capacity`] method to allocate the correct capacity initially:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" let mut s = String::with_capacity(25);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" println!(\"{}\", s.capacity());"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" for _ in 0..5 {"},{"DocComment":"     s.push_str(\"hello\");"},{"DocComment":"     println!(\"{}\", s.capacity());"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`with_capacity`]: String::with_capacity"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" We end up with a different output:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```text"},{"DocComment":" 25"},{"DocComment":" 25"},{"DocComment":" 25"},{"DocComment":" 25"},{"DocComment":" 25"},{"DocComment":" 25"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Here, there's no need to allocate more memory inside the loop."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [str]: prim@str \"str\""},{"DocComment":" [`str`]: prim@str \"str\""},{"DocComment":" [`&str`]: prim@str \"&str\""},{"DocComment":" [Deref]: core::ops::Deref \"ops::Deref\""},{"DocComment":" [`Deref`]: core::ops::Deref \"ops::Deref\""},{"DocComment":" [`as_str()`]: String::as_str"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"String"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":"Opaque","layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":24,"align":8,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[0],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},null,{"def_id":9,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["ptr",0]},{"Ident":["non_null",0]},{"Ident":["NonNull",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":13,"beg":{"line":74,"col":0},"end":{"line":74,"col":35}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" `*mut T` but non-zero and [covariant]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This is often the correct thing to use when building data structures using"},{"DocComment":" raw pointers, but is ultimately more dangerous to use because of its additional"},{"DocComment":" properties. If you're not sure if you should use `NonNull<T>`, just use `*mut T`!"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Unlike `*mut T`, the pointer must always be non-null, even if the pointer"},{"DocComment":" is never dereferenced. This is so that enums may use this forbidden value"},{"DocComment":" as a discriminant -- `Option<NonNull<T>>` has the same size as `*mut T`."},{"DocComment":" However the pointer may still dangle if it isn't dereferenced."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Unlike `*mut T`, `NonNull<T>` is covariant over `T`. This is usually the correct"},{"DocComment":" choice for most data structures and safe abstractions, such as `Box`, `Rc`, `Arc`, `Vec`,"},{"DocComment":" and `LinkedList`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In rare cases, if your type exposes a way to mutate the value of `T` through a `NonNull<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" and you need to prevent unsoundness from variance (for example, if `T` could be a reference"},{"DocComment":" with a shorter lifetime), you should add a field to make your type invariant, such as"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData<Cell<T>>` or `PhantomData<&'a mut T>`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Example of a type that must be invariant:"},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" use std::cell::Cell;"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" struct Invariant<T> {"},{"DocComment":"     ptr: std::ptr::NonNull<T>,"},{"DocComment":"     _invariant: PhantomData<Cell<T>>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Notice that `NonNull<T>` has a `From` instance for `&T`. However, this does"},{"DocComment":" not change the fact that mutating through a (pointer derived from a) shared"},{"DocComment":" reference is undefined behavior unless the mutation happens inside an"},{"DocComment":" [`UnsafeCell<T>`]. The same goes for creating a mutable reference from a shared"},{"DocComment":" reference. When using this `From` instance without an `UnsafeCell<T>`,"},{"DocComment":" it is your responsibility to ensure that `as_mut` is never called, and `as_ptr`"},{"DocComment":" is never used for mutation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Representation"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Thanks to the [null pointer optimization],"},{"DocComment":" `NonNull<T>` and `Option<NonNull<T>>`"},{"DocComment":" are guaranteed to have the same size and alignment:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::ptr::NonNull;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size_of::<NonNull<i16>>(), size_of::<Option<NonNull<i16>>>());"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(align_of::<NonNull<i16>>(), align_of::<Option<NonNull<i16>>>());"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(size_of::<NonNull<str>>(), size_of::<Option<NonNull<str>>>());"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(align_of::<NonNull<str>>(), align_of::<Option<NonNull<str>>>());"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [covariant]: https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/subtyping.html"},{"DocComment":" [`PhantomData`]: crate::marker::PhantomData"},{"DocComment":" [`UnsafeCell<T>`]: crate::cell::UnsafeCell"},{"DocComment":" [null pointer optimization]: crate::option#representation"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":"NonNull"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":13,"beg":{"line":75,"col":4},"end":{"line":75,"col":52}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"pointer","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[1261,{"Pattern":[{"HashConsedValue":[1260,{"RawPtr":[{"Deduplicated":1250},"Shared"]}]},"NotNull"]}]}}]},"layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":10,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["alloc",0]},{"Ident":["rc",0]},{"Ident":["RcInner",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":8,"beg":{"line":285,"col":0},"end":{"line":285,"col":25}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":8,"beg":{"line":286,"col":4},"end":{"line":286,"col":23}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"strong","ty":{"HashConsedValue":[955,{"Adt":{"id":{"Adt":12},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":679}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}]}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":8,"beg":{"line":287,"col":4},"end":{"line":287,"col":21}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"weak","ty":{"Deduplicated":955}},{"span":{"data":{"file_id":8,"beg":{"line":288,"col":4},"end":{"line":288,"col":12}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"value","ty":{"Deduplicated":1250}}]},"layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":11,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["marker",0]},{"Ident":["PhantomData",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":7,"beg":{"line":811,"col":0},"end":{"line":811,"col":39}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Zero-sized type used to mark things that \"act like\" they own a `T`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Adding a `PhantomData<T>` field to your type tells the compiler that your"},{"DocComment":" type acts as though it stores a value of type `T`, even though it doesn't"},{"DocComment":" really. This information is used when computing certain safety properties."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For a more in-depth explanation of how to use `PhantomData<T>`, please see"},{"DocComment":" [the Nomicon](../../nomicon/phantom-data.html)."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # A ghastly note 👻👻👻"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Though they both have scary names, `PhantomData` and 'phantom types' are"},{"DocComment":" related, but not identical. A phantom type parameter is simply a type"},{"DocComment":" parameter which is never used. In Rust, this often causes the compiler to"},{"DocComment":" complain, and the solution is to add a \"dummy\" use by way of `PhantomData`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused lifetime parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Perhaps the most common use case for `PhantomData` is a struct that has an"},{"DocComment":" unused lifetime parameter, typically as part of some unsafe code. For"},{"DocComment":" example, here is a struct `Slice` that has two pointers of type `*const T`,"},{"DocComment":" presumably pointing into an array somewhere:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```compile_fail,E0392"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The intention is that the underlying data is only valid for the"},{"DocComment":" lifetime `'a`, so `Slice` should not outlive `'a`. However, this"},{"DocComment":" intent is not expressed in the code, since there are no uses of"},{"DocComment":" the lifetime `'a` and hence it is not clear what data it applies"},{"DocComment":" to. We can correct this by telling the compiler to act *as if* the"},{"DocComment":" `Slice` struct contained a reference `&'a T`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # #[allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":"     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This also in turn infers the lifetime bound `T: 'a`, indicating"},{"DocComment":" that any references in `T` are valid over the lifetime `'a`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" When initializing a `Slice` you simply provide the value"},{"DocComment":" `PhantomData` for the field `phantom`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":" # struct Slice<'a, T> {"},{"DocComment":" #     start: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     end: *const T,"},{"DocComment":" #     phantom: PhantomData<&'a T>,"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" fn borrow_vec<T>(vec: &Vec<T>) -> Slice<'_, T> {"},{"DocComment":"     let ptr = vec.as_ptr();"},{"DocComment":"     Slice {"},{"DocComment":"         start: ptr,"},{"DocComment":"         end: unsafe { ptr.add(vec.len()) },"},{"DocComment":"         phantom: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Unused type parameters"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" It sometimes happens that you have unused type parameters which"},{"DocComment":" indicate what type of data a struct is \"tied\" to, even though that"},{"DocComment":" data is not actually found in the struct itself. Here is an"},{"DocComment":" example where this arises with [FFI]. The foreign interface uses"},{"DocComment":" handles of type `*mut ()` to refer to Rust values of different"},{"DocComment":" types. We track the Rust type using a phantom type parameter on"},{"DocComment":" the struct `ExternalResource` which wraps a handle."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [FFI]: ../../book/ch19-01-unsafe-rust.html#using-extern-functions-to-call-external-code"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" # #![allow(dead_code)]"},{"DocComment":" # trait ResType { }"},{"DocComment":" # struct ParamType;"},{"DocComment":" # mod foreign_lib {"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn new(_: usize) -> *mut () { 42 as *mut () }"},{"DocComment":" #     pub fn do_stuff(_: *mut (), _: usize) {}"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":" # fn convert_params(_: ParamType) -> usize { 42 }"},{"DocComment":" use std::marker::PhantomData;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct ExternalResource<R> {"},{"DocComment":"    resource_handle: *mut (),"},{"DocComment":"    resource_type: PhantomData<R>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl<R: ResType> ExternalResource<R> {"},{"DocComment":"     fn new() -> Self {"},{"DocComment":"         let size_of_res = size_of::<R>();"},{"DocComment":"         Self {"},{"DocComment":"             resource_handle: foreign_lib::new(size_of_res),"},{"DocComment":"             resource_type: PhantomData,"},{"DocComment":"         }"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":"     fn do_stuff(&self, param: ParamType) {"},{"DocComment":"         let foreign_params = convert_params(param);"},{"DocComment":"         foreign_lib::do_stuff(self.resource_handle, foreign_params);"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Ownership and the drop check"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The exact interaction of `PhantomData` with drop check **may change in the future**."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Currently, adding a field of type `PhantomData<T>` indicates that your type *owns* data of type"},{"DocComment":" `T` in very rare circumstances. This in turn has effects on the Rust compiler's [drop check]"},{"DocComment":" analysis. For the exact rules, see the [drop check] documentation."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ## Layout"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" For all `T`, the following are guaranteed:"},{"DocComment":" * `size_of::<PhantomData<T>>() == 0`"},{"DocComment":" * `align_of::<PhantomData<T>>() == 1`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [drop check]: Drop#drop-check"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"phantom_data"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[]},"layout":[{"key":"x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu","value":{"size":0,"align":1,"discriminator":{"Known":0},"uninhabited":false,"variant_layouts":[{"field_offsets":[],"uninhabited":false,"tagger":[]}],"repr":{"repr_algo":"Rust","align_modif":null,"transparent":false,"explicit_discr_type":false}}}],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":12,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["cell",0]},{"Ident":["Cell",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":10,"beg":{"line":312,"col":0},"end":{"line":312,"col":26}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" A mutable memory location."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Memory layout"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `Cell<T>` has the same [memory layout and caveats as"},{"DocComment":" `UnsafeCell<T>`](UnsafeCell#memory-layout). In particular, this means that"},{"DocComment":" `Cell<T>` has the same in-memory representation as its inner type `T`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" In this example, you can see that `Cell<T>` enables mutation inside an"},{"DocComment":" immutable struct. In other words, it enables \"interior mutability\"."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::cell::Cell;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" struct SomeStruct {"},{"DocComment":"     regular_field: u8,"},{"DocComment":"     special_field: Cell<u8>,"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let my_struct = SomeStruct {"},{"DocComment":"     regular_field: 0,"},{"DocComment":"     special_field: Cell::new(1),"},{"DocComment":" };"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let new_value = 100;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // ERROR: `my_struct` is immutable"},{"DocComment":" // my_struct.regular_field = new_value;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // WORKS: although `my_struct` is immutable, `special_field` is a `Cell`,"},{"DocComment":" // which can always be mutated"},{"DocComment":" my_struct.special_field.set(new_value);"},{"DocComment":" assert_eq!(my_struct.special_field.get(), new_value);"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" See the [module-level documentation](self) for more."}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Transparent","lang_item":"Cell"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"src":"TopLevel","kind":{"Struct":[{"span":{"data":{"file_id":10,"beg":{"line":313,"col":4},"end":{"line":313,"col":24}},"generated_from_span":null},"attr_info":{"attributes":[],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":false},"name":"value","ty":{"Deduplicated":1259}}]},"layout":[],"ptr_metadata":"None"},{"def_id":13,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["cell",0]},{"Ident":["UnsafeCell",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":10,"beg":{"line":2323,"col":0},"end":{"line":2323,"col":32}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" The core primitive for interior mutability in Rust."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" If you have a reference `&T`, then normally in Rust the compiler performs optimizations based on"},{"DocComment":" the knowledge that `&T` points to immutable data. Mutating that data, for example through an"},{"DocComment":" alias or by transmuting a `&T` into a `&mut T`, is considered undefined behavior."},{"DocComment":" `UnsafeCell<T>` opts-out of the immutability guarantee for `&T`: a shared reference"},{"DocComment":" `&UnsafeCell<T>` may point to data that is being mutated. This is called \"interior mutability\"."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" All other types that allow internal mutability, such as [`Cell<T>`] and [`RefCell<T>`], internally"},{"DocComment":" use `UnsafeCell` to wrap their data."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that only the immutability guarantee for shared references is affected by `UnsafeCell`. The"},{"DocComment":" uniqueness guarantee for mutable references is unaffected. There is *no* legal way to obtain"},{"DocComment":" aliasing `&mut`, not even with `UnsafeCell<T>`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `UnsafeCell` does nothing to avoid data races; they are still undefined behavior. If multiple"},{"DocComment":" threads have access to the same `UnsafeCell`, they must follow the usual rules of the"},{"DocComment":" [concurrent memory model]: conflicting non-synchronized accesses must be done via the APIs in"},{"DocComment":" [`core::sync::atomic`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The `UnsafeCell` API itself is technically very simple: [`.get()`] gives you a raw pointer"},{"DocComment":" `*mut T` to its contents. It is up to _you_ as the abstraction designer to use that raw pointer"},{"DocComment":" correctly."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`.get()`]: `UnsafeCell::get`"},{"DocComment":" [concurrent memory model]: ../sync/atomic/index.html#memory-model-for-atomic-accesses"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Aliasing rules"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The precise Rust aliasing rules are somewhat in flux, but the main points are not contentious:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" - If you create a safe reference with lifetime `'a` (either a `&T` or `&mut T` reference), then"},{"DocComment":"   you must not access the data in any way that contradicts that reference for the remainder of"},{"DocComment":"   `'a`. For example, this means that if you take the `*mut T` from an `UnsafeCell<T>` and cast it"},{"DocComment":"   to an `&T`, then the data in `T` must remain immutable (modulo any `UnsafeCell` data found"},{"DocComment":"   within `T`, of course) until that reference's lifetime expires. Similarly, if you create a"},{"DocComment":"   `&mut T` reference that is released to safe code, then you must not access the data within the"},{"DocComment":"   `UnsafeCell` until that reference expires."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" - For both `&T` without `UnsafeCell<_>` and `&mut T`, you must also not deallocate the data"},{"DocComment":"   until the reference expires. As a special exception, given an `&T`, any part of it that is"},{"DocComment":"   inside an `UnsafeCell<_>` may be deallocated during the lifetime of the reference, after the"},{"DocComment":"   last time the reference is used (dereferenced or reborrowed). Since you cannot deallocate a part"},{"DocComment":"   of what a reference points to, this means the memory an `&T` points to can be deallocated only if"},{"DocComment":"   *every part of it* (including padding) is inside an `UnsafeCell`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" However, whenever a `&UnsafeCell<T>` is constructed or dereferenced, it must still point to"},{"DocComment":" live memory and the compiler is allowed to insert spurious reads if it can prove that this"},{"DocComment":" memory has not yet been deallocated."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" To assist with proper design, the following scenarios are explicitly declared legal"},{"DocComment":" for single-threaded code:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" 1. A `&T` reference can be released to safe code and there it can co-exist with other `&T`"},{"DocComment":"    references, but not with a `&mut T`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" 2. A `&mut T` reference may be released to safe code provided neither other `&mut T` nor `&T`"},{"DocComment":"    co-exist with it. A `&mut T` must always be unique."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that whilst mutating the contents of an `&UnsafeCell<T>` (even while other"},{"DocComment":" `&UnsafeCell<T>` references alias the cell) is"},{"DocComment":" ok (provided you enforce the above invariants some other way), it is still undefined behavior"},{"DocComment":" to have multiple `&mut UnsafeCell<T>` aliases. That is, `UnsafeCell` is a wrapper"},{"DocComment":" designed to have a special interaction with _shared_ accesses (_i.e._, through an"},{"DocComment":" `&UnsafeCell<_>` reference); there is no magic whatsoever when dealing with _exclusive_"},{"DocComment":" accesses (_e.g._, through a `&mut UnsafeCell<_>`): neither the cell nor the wrapped value"},{"DocComment":" may be aliased for the duration of that `&mut` borrow."},{"DocComment":" This is showcased by the [`.get_mut()`] accessor, which is a _safe_ getter that yields"},{"DocComment":" a `&mut T`."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`.get_mut()`]: `UnsafeCell::get_mut`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Memory layout"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" `UnsafeCell<T>` has the same in-memory representation as its inner type `T`. A consequence"},{"DocComment":" of this guarantee is that it is possible to convert between `T` and `UnsafeCell<T>`."},{"DocComment":" Special care has to be taken when converting a nested `T` inside of an `Outer<T>` type"},{"DocComment":" to an `Outer<UnsafeCell<T>>` type: this is not sound when the `Outer<T>` type enables [niche]"},{"DocComment":" optimizations. For example, the type `Option<NonNull<u8>>` is typically 8 bytes large on"},{"DocComment":" 64-bit platforms, but the type `Option<UnsafeCell<NonNull<u8>>>` takes up 16 bytes of space."},{"DocComment":" Therefore this is not a valid conversion, despite `NonNull<u8>` and `UnsafeCell<NonNull<u8>>>`"},{"DocComment":" having the same memory layout. This is because `UnsafeCell` disables niche optimizations in"},{"DocComment":" order to avoid its interior mutability property from spreading from `T` into the `Outer` type,"},{"DocComment":" thus this can cause distortions in the type size in these cases."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that the only valid way to obtain a `*mut T` pointer to the contents of a"},{"DocComment":" _shared_ `UnsafeCell<T>` is through [`.get()`]  or [`.raw_get()`]. A `&mut T` reference"},{"DocComment":" can be obtained by either dereferencing this pointer or by calling [`.get_mut()`]"},{"DocComment":" on an _exclusive_ `UnsafeCell<T>`. Even though `T` and `UnsafeCell<T>` have the"},{"DocComment":" same memory layout, the following is not allowed and undefined behavior:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust,compile_fail"},{"DocComment":" # use std::cell::UnsafeCell;"},{"DocComment":" unsafe fn not_allowed<T>(ptr: &UnsafeCell<T>) -> &mut T {"},{"DocComment":"   let t = ptr as *const UnsafeCell<T> as *mut T;"},{"DocComment":"   // This is undefined behavior, because the `*mut T` pointer"},{"DocComment":"   // was not obtained through `.get()` nor `.raw_get()`:"},{"DocComment":"   unsafe { &mut *t }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Instead, do this:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" # use std::cell::UnsafeCell;"},{"DocComment":" // Safety: the caller must ensure that there are no references that"},{"DocComment":" // point to the *contents* of the `UnsafeCell`."},{"DocComment":" unsafe fn get_mut<T>(ptr: &UnsafeCell<T>) -> &mut T {"},{"DocComment":"   unsafe { &mut *ptr.get() }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Converting in the other direction from a `&mut T`"},{"DocComment":" to an `&UnsafeCell<T>` is allowed:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" # use std::cell::UnsafeCell;"},{"DocComment":" fn get_shared<T>(ptr: &mut T) -> &UnsafeCell<T> {"},{"DocComment":"   let t = ptr as *mut T as *const UnsafeCell<T>;"},{"DocComment":"   // SAFETY: `T` and `UnsafeCell<T>` have the same memory layout"},{"DocComment":"   unsafe { &*t }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [niche]: https://rust-lang.github.io/unsafe-code-guidelines/glossary.html#niche"},{"DocComment":" [`.raw_get()`]: `UnsafeCell::raw_get`"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Here is an example showcasing how to soundly mutate the contents of an `UnsafeCell<_>` despite"},{"DocComment":" there being multiple references aliasing the cell:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::cell::UnsafeCell;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let x: UnsafeCell<i32> = 42.into();"},{"DocComment":" // Get multiple / concurrent / shared references to the same `x`."},{"DocComment":" let (p1, p2): (&UnsafeCell<i32>, &UnsafeCell<i32>) = (&x, &x);"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     // SAFETY: within this scope there are no other references to `x`'s contents,"},{"DocComment":"     // so ours is effectively unique."},{"DocComment":"     let p1_exclusive: &mut i32 = &mut *p1.get(); // -- borrow --+"},{"DocComment":"     *p1_exclusive += 27; //                                     |"},{"DocComment":" } // <---------- cannot go beyond this point -------------------+"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"     // SAFETY: within this scope nobody expects to have exclusive access to `x`'s contents,"},{"DocComment":"     // so we can have multiple shared accesses concurrently."},{"DocComment":"     let p2_shared: &i32 = &*p2.get();"},{"DocComment":"     assert_eq!(*p2_shared, 42 + 27);"},{"DocComment":"     let p1_shared: &i32 = &*p1.get();"},{"DocComment":"     assert_eq!(*p1_shared, *p2_shared);"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" The following example showcases the fact that exclusive access to an `UnsafeCell<T>`"},{"DocComment":" implies exclusive access to its `T`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```rust"},{"DocComment":" #![forbid(unsafe_code)]"},{"DocComment":" // with exclusive accesses, `UnsafeCell` is a transparent no-op wrapper, so no need for"},{"DocComment":" // `unsafe` here."},{"DocComment":" use std::cell::UnsafeCell;"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" let mut x: UnsafeCell<i32> = 42.into();"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" // Get a compile-time-checked unique reference to `x`."},{"DocComment":" let p_unique: &mut UnsafeCell<i32> = &mut x;"},{"DocComment":" // With an exclusive reference, we can mutate the contents for free."},{"DocComment":" *p_uni